    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">What Changes About the Mortgage Process the Second Time You Buy a Home</h1>

    <p class="mt-8">If you've bought a home before, you know more about the mortgage process than a first-time buyer walking in cold. You also carry a set of assumptions built from that first experience that may not serve you as well the second time. The market changes. Your financial profile changes. The products available change. And the specific complication of buying while already owning a home adds a layer that didn't exist the first time. This article is about the gaps between what repeat buyers think they know and what they need to find out again.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Assumption That Costs the Most: Rate Expectations From Last Time</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">The most expensive assumption repeat buyers carry is that the process will feel like it did before, including the rate. If your first mortgage closed in a different rate environment, your mental benchmark for what a mortgage costs may be significantly off from current market pricing. Buyers who anchor to a rate they paid three or five years ago tend to shop less aggressively than they should, accept the first quote they receive because it doesn't shock them, and miss the variance across lenders that makes rate shopping worth doing.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Rates on your second purchase should be evaluated as if you've never seen a mortgage rate before. The only number that matters is the current market, and the only way to know where you sit in that market is to get quotes from multiple lenders on the same day with the same loan parameters.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Hidden Complication: Your Financial Profile Has Changed</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Here's what many repeat buyers underestimate. The income, debt, and asset picture that qualified you for your first mortgage may look meaningfully different today. Career changes, business ownership, additional debt, deferred student loans that have entered repayment, a gap in employment, any of those shifts can affect how a lender evaluates your application. A buyer who qualified easily at 30 may face a more complex process at 38 if their financial life has become more complicated in the intervening years.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Pulling your credit and calculating your current DTI before you start the process tells you where you actually stand, rather than where you assume you stand based on how easily the first purchase went.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What the Documentation Process Looks Like With an Existing Mortgage</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Your first mortgage application required documentation of your income, assets, and debts. Your second requires all of that plus documentation of your existing mortgage, your current home's value, and a clear picture of how you plan to handle the departing residence. If you're selling, lenders will want evidence of that plan. If you're converting to a rental, they'll want a lease. If you're keeping the property without a clear plan documented, the full existing mortgage payment stays in your DTI.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Repeat buyers who show up to their second application with the same documentation mindset as their first one often find the process moving more slowly than they expected, not because their profile is weaker, but because the file requires more pieces that no one told them to gather in advance.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Pull your credit and calculate your current DTI before you talk to any lender.</strong> Know your actual financial picture, not the one from several years ago. Your score, your obligations, and your income may all have shifted in ways that affect your qualification.</li>
      <li><strong>Get rate quotes from at least three lenders before you choose one.</strong> Repeat buyers who rely on their previous lender out of convenience rather than comparison frequently leave money on the table. The relationship doesn't guarantee the best rate.</li>
      <li><strong>Gather documentation of your existing mortgage before your application.</strong> Your most recent mortgage statement, your current balance, and your monthly payment amount should all be in hand before your lender asks for them.</li>
      <li><strong>Have a clear and documented plan for your departing residence before you apply.</strong> Sale, rental, or bridge, the plan needs to exist and be supportable with documentation before your lender can treat your existing mortgage favorably in the DTI calculation.</li>
      <li><strong>Ask your lender how the process differs from a first purchase given your specific situation.</strong> A lender who regularly works with move-up buyers will proactively identify the additional steps. One who doesn't may not raise them until underwriting does.</li>
    </ol>

